@* Fieldset's Overview, written by hand after the form field Overviews: a label and description,
   columns, a list of settings with dividers, and icons on sections (each a form previewed in each
   mode). Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A fieldset is a group of fields under a heading, in one part of a form. It suits an address or a set of notification settings. Screen readers announce that heading as the group's name when focus moves into it. A <Anchor Href="docs/components/radio-group">RadioGroup</Anchor> or <Anchor Href="docs/components/checkbox-group">CheckboxGroup</Anchor> is a group already, so it needs no Fieldset of its own. For a part of a page that isn't a form, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/card">Card</Anchor> or <Anchor Href="docs/components/section">Section</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Label and description</Heading>
<TextBlock>A fieldset has a heading, and can have a short line of description under it. Set <Code>Label</Code> to the heading; every fieldset needs one. To add a description, set <Code>Description</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="fieldset-address" />
<TextBlock>Screen readers can read the description with the group's name. Help for one field still goes in that field's <Code>Hint</Code>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Columns</Heading>
<TextBlock>A fieldset can lay its fields out side by side in columns, as a <Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor> does. Set <Code>Columns</Code> to 1 to 4. A field fills one column; for the whole row, set <Code>ColSpan="GridSpan.Full"</Code> on it.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="fieldset-columns" />
<List>
    <ListItem>The columns follow the group's own width: two from 32rem and three from 48rem. So a narrow group shows fewer columns than <Code>Columns</Code> sets; this example shows two until the page is wide.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>In a Form with columns, a Fieldset fills the whole row and lays out its own fields.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">A list of settings</Heading>
<TextBlock>A fieldset can show its rows as a list with a line between each, as in a list of settings. Set <Code>Dividers</Code> to turn it on. To set the space above and below each line, set <Code>Gap</Code>. The default is 4.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="fieldset-dividers" />
<TextBlock>Each direct child is a row, so <Code>Dividers</Code> replaces <Code>Divider</Code> components between them. It's ignored with more than one column.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Icons on sections</Heading>
<TextBlock>On a page of several groups, an icon at the start of each heading helps the user tell the sections apart at a glance. Set <Code>Icon</Code> to add one. The icon sits beside the label and description, and is decorative. Put icons on the sections or on the rows inside them, not both.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="fieldset-icons" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <Form Model="Model" FormName="checkout">
            <Fieldset Label="Shipping address" Description="Where we send your order.">
                <TextField @bind-Value="Model.Street" />
                <TextField @bind-Value="Model.City" />
            </Fieldset>
        </Form>
        """;
}
